\begin{abstract}
We predict, at 05:00 on the day of a flight, whether a New York departure will arrive at least 15 minutes late, using only the published schedule, static aircraft data and the delays of earlier days. On <<n(d1['summary']['flights'])>> flights from 2013 we follow the full machine-learning life cycle: problem definition, data collection, EDA, preprocessing, a chronological split, leakage-safe feature engineering and selection, baselines, model selection, honest tuning of gradient boosting with rolling months, and a single evaluation on November--December (base rate <<f3(d5['test_base_rate'])>>). Tuned XGBoost (test PR AUC <<f3(GB['pr_auc'])>>), logistic regression (<<f3(LR['pr_auc'])>>) and a hybrid that boosts from the logistic-regression prediction (<<f3(HY['pr_auc'])>>) all beat a majority-class (<<f3(MAJ['pr_auc'])>>) and a rule-of-thumb baseline (<<f3(RULE['pr_auc'])>>), but their 95\% confidence intervals overlap: at this decision time gradient boosting does not significantly beat a regularised linear model. We show why: early stopping on the scored month inflates boosting's validation score, the useful tree count is unstable between months, and tree splits on absolute delay-rate levels suffer from seasonal drift. An appendix records every design decision, the evidence for it and what happened.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Late arrivals cascade through crews, gates and connections. A risk score available before the first departure lets planners add buffer or warn passengers. Following the course's five-step blueprint, the goal is to rank flights by delay risk (PR AUC) and to produce a flag list with precision $\geq 0.5$. The problem is binary classification: the U.S.\ BTS defines a flight as delayed when it arrives 15 or more minutes late, and delay minutes are heavily skewed (skewness <<f2(d2['arr_delay_skew'])>>). The project brief asks for gradient boosting; we test whether it is justified against simple baselines, as Phase 7 of the life cycle requires. The work is organised as five notebooks that follow the course's life-cycle phases; each writes its own module in \texttt{src/fdp/} and saves its results to \texttt{reports/}, from which every number in this report is generated.

\section{Related Work}
\textbf{Flight delay prediction.} Sternberg et al.\ \cite{sternberg2017} survey statistical, network-based and machine-learning approaches to flight delay prediction. Rebollo and Balakrishnan \cite{rebollo2014} show that the current delay state of the air-traffic network is a strong predictor of future delays, using random forests over temporal and network delay-state features; our trailing delay-history features follow the same idea, restricted to what is known at 05:00. Gui et al.\ \cite{gui2020} compare an LSTM with a random forest on aviation big data, a direction we keep for Phase 2. A recurring weakness in this literature is that inputs observed after the decision time, or random train/test splits, make results look better than an operational system could achieve; our fixed decision time, leakage test and chronological split address this directly.

\textbf{Methods.} Gradient boosting fits additive trees to the negative gradient of a loss \cite{friedman2001}; XGBoost adds second-order leaf values, regularisation and histogram splits \cite{chen2016}. Platt scaling \cite{platt1999} and the calibration study of Niculescu-Mizil and Caruana \cite{niculescu2005} motivate checking probabilities, not only ranking. Re-sampling such as SMOTE \cite{chawla2002} changes the class ratio the model sees, which is why we do not use it.

\textbf{Evaluation.} With a minority positive class, the precision--recall curve is more informative than ROC \cite{saito2015}, so PR AUC is our main metric. Rolling-origin evaluation \cite{tashman2000} and avoidance of leakage \cite{kaufman2012} shape our tuning protocol, and the block bootstrap \cite{kunsch1989} gives intervals for dependent (same-day) observations.

\section{Problem, Data and Split (Phases 1, 2, 5)}
\textbf{Decision time.} Inputs must be known at 05:00 on day D: the published schedule for D, static aircraft facts and outcomes of earlier days. We first considered ``end of the previous day'', but about 3\% of the previous day's flights land after midnight, so 05:00 is when that history is really complete. Departure and arrival times, delays and air time are never inputs.

\textbf{Data.} The Kaggle \emph{Flights} tables (nycflights13 \cite{wickham2013}) contain <<n(d1['summary']['flights'])>> scheduled departures from JFK, LGA and EWR plus a planes table. <<n(d1['summary']['cancelled'])>> cancelled and <<n(d1['summary']['diverted'])>> diverted flights have no arrival delay, so their label is undefined; they are kept on the schedule (they count towards congestion) but never labelled, leaving <<n(d1['summary']['labelled'])>> labelled flights, <<f3(d1['summary']['delayed_share_of_labelled'])>> of them delayed.

\textbf{Metric first.} The metric was fixed before any model: PR AUC (average precision), with ROC AUC, Brier score, expected calibration error (ECE), lift = PR AUC / base rate, and 95\% confidence intervals from a day-block bootstrap (flights on one day share weather, so days are resampled). Accuracy is rejected: predicting ``always on time'' scores <<f3(d1['valid_always_on_time_accuracy'])>> accuracy in September and catches no delays.

\textbf{Split.} The split is chronological (Table~\ref{tab:split}); January 1--14 only feeds history features. The monthly delay rate moves from <<f3(min(d1['monthly_delay_rate'].values()))>> (September) to <<f3(max(d1['monthly_delay_rate'].values()))>> (December, Fig.~\ref{fig:split}). This base-rate shift recurs in every later result.

