\section{Features and Selection (Phase 6)}
We build <<d3['n_numeric']>> numeric features in five groups plus carrier, origin and destination: cyclic (sin/cos) schedule time, calendar (day of week, weekend, days to a federal holiday), aircraft, congestion counted from the published schedule, and trailing delay history for 12 (grouping, window) pairs. History rates are shrunk towards the overall rate, $\hat r_g=(k_g+\alpha\,r_0)/(n_g+\alpha)$ with $\alpha=<<d3['alpha']>>$, and each comes with a log-volume feature. Windows end on day D$-$1 (a rolling sum followed by a one-day shift). A unit test scrambles every outcome from a cutoff date onward and checks that features on that date are unchanged; it passes. Month and day-of-year are deliberately excluded: test months never occur in training, so they would act as date IDs.

Adding groups one at a time to logistic regression, all five together score best on both September (<<f3(grp[4]['valid_pr_auc'])>>) and October (<<f3(grp[4]['calib_pr_auc'])>>). History gives the largest September gain (+<<f3(grp[4]['valid_pr_auc'] - grp[3]['valid_pr_auc'])>>) but almost none in October (+<<f3(grp[4]['calib_pr_auc'] - grp[3]['calib_pr_auc'])>>): which group matters depends on the month.

We tested five selection methods and adopt one only if it beats all features on \emph{both} held-out months (Table~\ref{tab:sel}). None does. Sequential forward selection, written from scratch, reached <<f3(sfs['valid_sfs'])>> on September, the month it selected on, but fell to <<f3(sfs['calib_sfs'])>> on October against <<f3(sfs['calib_all'])>> for all features: it over-fitted its selection month. <<d3['vif']['n_above_10']>> features have VIF $>10$, yet pruning them lowered both months, because collinearity harms coefficient interpretation, not prediction. PCA needs <<d3['pca_k95']>> components for 95\% variance. All features are kept.

