\section{Baselines and Model Selection (Phases 7, 8A)}
Baselines on September / October: majority class <<f3(d4['baselines']['valid']['majority_class'])>> / <<f3(d4['baselines']['calib']['majority_class'])>>; a planner's rule, the flight number's 28-day delay rate, <<f3(d4['baselines']['valid']['rule_flight_28d_rate'])>> / <<f3(d4['baselines']['calib']['rule_flight_28d_rate'])>>; scheduled hour <<f3(d4['baselines']['valid']['rule_scheduled_hour'])>> / <<f3(d4['baselines']['calib']['rule_scheduled_hour'])>>.

Logistic regression was implemented from scratch as maximum-likelihood estimation by gradient descent, $\nabla L = X^\top(\sigma(Xw)-y)/n + \lambda w$, with batch, mini-batch (256) and stochastic updates. Batch GD reached a training log loss of <<gd['batch GD (lr 0.5)']['final_train_logloss']>> against <<gd['scikit-learn (lbfgs, same model)']['final_train_logloss']>> for scikit-learn. A learning rate of 0.01 was slow (<<d4['lr_effect_logloss']['0.01']>> after 100 epochs) and 8 diverged (<<d4['lr_effect_logloss']['8.0']>>). The L2 strength chosen on September is $C=<<d4['best_C']>>$; train and September scores hardly change with $C$, so the linear model is limited by bias, not variance. A single decision tree memorises: unlimited depth scores <<f3(depth['None']['train_pr_auc'])>> on train and <<f3(depth['None']['valid_pr_auc'])>> on September.

Model families were compared on rolling months (train on all months before July, August or September; score that month) with fixed, untuned settings (Table~\ref{tab:family}). Logistic regression led; untuned XGBoost was third. Since default hyper-parameters are not a fair test of boosting, it proceeds to honest tuning.

